# kanpai — copy to `.env.local` and adjust. `.env.local` is gitignored.
#
# The local loop needs nothing hosted. It runs on a Postgres in Docker that
# `pnpm db:up` creates, and there is no Google OAuth client to make and no cloud
# account to open. Running a copy in public is the README's *Running your own copy*
# (ADR-0008).

# ── The one variable, and the only one ────────────────────────────────────────
# `DATABASE_URL` carries the host, the port, the credentials and the database
# name, and everything reads it: `next dev`, `pnpm db:*` and `pnpm test`. It is
# also what the container is built from, so changing the port here is the whole
# change — `pnpm db:up` publishes whatever port this names and names the
# container after it (`kanpai-pg` on 5432, `kanpai-pg-5433` on 5433), so two
# checkouts on different ports never collide.
#
# Change it if something already listens on 5432 — a Postgres installed on the
# host, or tsundoku's own `tsundoku-pg`, most often — or if a second checkout needs
# its own database. `pnpm test` uses the same server and the same name with `_test`
# appended, which it creates.
#
# A value set in the shell wins over this file, so `DATABASE_URL=… pnpm test`
# works without editing anything.
#
# `docker compose up` ignores this value and sets its own, pointing at its own
# `postgres` service — see `compose.yaml`.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://kanpai:kanpai@127.0.0.1:5432/kanpai

# ── The owner gate ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The web view is gated by Google, restricted to one address (ADR-0001, ADR-0008).
# **None of the four variables that boot the real gate carries a value in this file
# and none ever should**: three of them are secrets, and the fourth is a person. The
# one variable set below is not one of them — it is the local opt-in that stands in
# for the gate while there is nothing to sign in to.
#
# Locally there is no Google OAuth client, so the local loop runs with the gate open,
# by an act rather than by the absence of a configuration:
#
#   AUTH_DEV_OPEN=true
#
# It is never honoured in a production build — the image runs one — so it cannot be
# the reason the owner's record ends up readable from the internet. With it set,
# Auth.js is not reached at all: no client id, no secret, no `AUTH_SECRET`.
#
# **Running a copy with `docker compose up`? Delete this line** and fill in the four
# below: the image ignores it anyway, and leaving it here says the opposite of what
# the copy does.
AUTH_DEV_OPEN=true

# The four variables that boot the real gate, once there is a Google OAuth client
# to point at. Leave them unset until then; the gate fails closed without them, and
# a deployment missing one refuses the owner rather than admitting everybody.
#
#   AUTH_SECRET          what the session cookie is signed and encrypted with.
#                        `pnpm dlx auth secret` (or `openssl rand -base64 33`) prints
#                        one. Rotating it is the kill switch: it invalidates every
#                        session at once.
#   AUTH_GOOGLE_ID       the OAuth client id from Google Cloud Console.
#   AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET   its secret.
#   AUTH_OWNER_EMAIL     the one Google address that may sign in. Singular: a
#                        comma in this value is a misconfiguration, not a list, and
#                        the gate refuses it rather than admitting the first name.
#
# The authorised redirect URI to register with Google is
# `https://<domain>/api/auth/callback/google`, and `http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google`
# if you want to exercise the real gate locally or through `docker compose up`.
#
# AUTH_SECRET=
# AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
# AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=
# AUTH_OWNER_EMAIL=you@example.com

# ── The MCP door ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No variable. `/mcp` takes access tokens the owner issues, which are rows in the
# database and not configuration (ADR-0009): `pnpm token:issue <name>` locally,
# `docker compose exec app node --experimental-strip-types db/token.ts issue <name>`
# in a running copy. With none issued, every request to `/mcp` is refused.

# ── Where the copy is reached ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The public origin, which Auth.js builds the Google redirect from. It must match
# the authorised redirect URI registered with Google exactly. `compose.yaml`
# defaults it to `http://localhost:3000`; set it to your own domain once the copy
# sits behind a TLS terminator.
#
# AUTH_URL=https://kanpai.example.com

# ── The Sources ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where `search_catalogue` looks for a bottle the catalogue does not have yet,
# after the owner's own Labels (ADR-0011). Asked only on an explicit search,
# never on a page render; images are hotlinked, never stored.
#
# Open Food Facts is on by default: open data (images CC BY-SA, data ODbL) whose
# terms invite this use. Set this to `false` for a copy that should make no
# outbound call at all; the search then answers from the owner's Labels only.
#
# SOURCES_OPENFOODFACTS=false

# Vivino — **off unless you set both variables below.** Read this first:
#
#   - **Vivino's terms of service forbid automated access** ("through automated
#     means, scraping tools, crawlers, or bots"). Turning this on uses Vivino
#     outside its terms. It is your call for your copy, and nobody else's.
#   - kanpai asks only the Algolia index behind vivino.com's own search box, with
#     the public, search-only key that page ships, and an honest User-Agent. It
#     never calls vivino.com itself and never pretends to be a browser.
#   - The key is Vivino's and **may rotate** without notice. When it stops
#     working the search says Vivino is unavailable and goes on with Open Food
#     Facts and what you type; nothing else breaks.
#   - The key is not in this repository and never will be. It is in vivino.com's
#     navigation script, as `algoliasearch("<app id>", "<key>")` next to
#     `initIndex("WINES_prod")` — `docs/research/sources.md` §8 has the commands.
#
# Why bother: on wine Vivino is the better catalogue by far (14 of 14 of the
# owner's wines, with a clean bottle shot, against 2 of 14 on Open Food Facts).
#
# VIVINO_ALGOLIA_APP_ID=
# VIVINO_ALGOLIA_KEY=
